= Belief — Technical =

== Propositional structure ==
Belief is often represented as a relation:

<pre>
B_s(p)
</pre>

meaning subject <code>s</code> believes proposition <code>p</code>.

Doxastic logic extends modal logic with operators representing belief and studies closure, consistency, introspection, and idealization of belief sets.

Real cognitive agents do not satisfy every ideal logical closure property; logical models are normative or structural abstractions rather than complete psychological theories.

== Categorical belief and credence ==
Categorical belief treats attitude as discrete. Bayesian models instead use a credence function:

<pre>
Cr_s(p) ∈ [0,1]
</pre>

subject to probabilistic coherence constraints under standard Bayesian norms.

The relation between categorical belief and credence is disputed. Threshold accounts propose belief when credence exceeds some cutoff, but simple fixed thresholds create problems under conjunction and context change.

== Representationalism ==
Representational theories characterize belief through internal states carrying semantic content.

Questions include:
* what realizes the representation;
* whether content is symbolic, distributed, structural, or embodied;
* whether belief content is individuated internally or partly by external environment;
* whether stored representations are necessary for all belief attribution.

== Dispositionalism ==
Dispositional theories analyze belief in terms of tendencies to infer, assert, act, feel surprise, or respond cognitively under relevant circumstances.

A challenge is that dispositions can be masked, overridden, context-dependent, or internally inconsistent.

== Interpretationism ==
Interpretationist approaches treat belief attribution as part of a rationalizing/explanatory scheme used to make sense of behavior.

Hard instrumentalist variants deny robust underlying belief states; weaker views allow belief reality while emphasizing interpretation.

== Belief revision ==
Formal belief-revision theories study how a belief set should change under new information.

Core operations often include:
* expansion;
* contraction;
* revision.

Bayesian conditionalization provides a graded analogue for updating credences, but real inquiry also involves model change, discovery of new possibilities, uncertain evidence, and source reliability.

